          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Install Polymeric Sand: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Installing polymeric sand is simple, but a few steps are non-negotiable — skip them and you risk a weak joint or a permanent white haze. Here's the process that gives professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Fill the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Make sure the pavers and joints are <strong>completely dry</strong>, then pour the polymeric sand onto the surface and sweep it into the joints with a stiff broom. Work it in until every joint is full and firm right up near the surface — under-filled joints are weak joints. Don't fill on a damp day or with rain in the forecast, because moisture pre-activates the polymers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Sweep Off ALL the Excess</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step that makes or breaks the job. Sweep every last grain of sand off the paver faces, then go over the whole surface with a leaf blower held at a low, shallow angle to clear the dust from the textures and chamfers. <strong>Any polymer dust left on the pavers will turn into a white haze</strong> the moment it gets wet. Be obsessive here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Compact the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Lightly tamp or run a plate compactor (with a protective mat) over the pavers to settle the sand and remove air pockets. Top up any joints that drop, sweep and blow the surface clean again, and confirm the joints are filled to just below the paver chamfer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Mist-Activate With Water</h3>
          <p>
            Set your hose nozzle to a gentle <strong>shower or mist</strong> — never a hard stream. Lightly water each section just until the joints are saturated but not flooded, working in small areas so you can control it. Over-watering floats the polymers up onto the paver faces and causes haze and weak joints; under-watering leaves the joint uncured. Follow the soak-and-pause pattern on the bag.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Let It Cure & Avoid Haze</h3>
          <p>
            Keep all traffic and water off the surface for the full cure time on the label — typically <strong>24 hours</strong> before light foot traffic and longer before heavy use or rain. If you do spot a faint haze after curing, a haze remover made for polymeric sand can usually clear it, but the real cure is prevention: dry surface, full joints, clean faces, gentle mist.
          </p>
        </div>

