          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Polymeric Sand Colors & Troubleshooting Haze
          </h2>
          <p>
            Polymeric sand isn't just functional — the color matters for how the finished surface looks. Most brands offer at least a <strong>tan/buff</strong> and a <strong>gray</strong>, while premium lines from SureBond and DOMINATOR add several shades in between. <strong>Gray polymeric sand</strong> is the most popular choice today because it blends cleanly with concrete pavers, bluestone, and modern dark-toned hardscapes, while tan suits traditional clay brick. Pick a color one step darker than you think you want — joints read lighter once dry — and always buy enough of a single lot so the color is consistent across the whole patio.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most common complaint isn't color, though — it's <strong>haze</strong>, the milky white film that can cloud the paver faces after activation. Haze comes from polymer dust left on the surface that gels when wet. If you catch it early, scrubbing with water and a stiff brush can lift fresh residue; for set-in haze, a dedicated polymeric sand haze remover dissolves the film without harming the joints. But every experienced installer will tell you the same thing: haze is almost entirely preventable. Fill on a dry day, sweep and blow the paver faces spotless before adding water, mist gently instead of flooding, and you'll get clean joints with no film at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two other quick troubleshooting notes. <strong>Crumbling or weak joints</strong> almost always mean under-watering during activation or sand that got rained on before it cured — re-sand and re-activate those joints. And <strong>joints that never hardened</strong> usually weren't misted enough; polymeric sand needs real water penetration to set, so don't be shy with the mist (just don't flood it).
          </p>
